<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Suomussalmi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1939">1939</a>–<a href="/years/1940">1940</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-war">Winter War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Suomussalmi was fought in the forests of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> from December 1939 to January 1940, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-war">Winter War</a>. Finnish ski troops under Colonel Hjalmar Siilasvuo cut the road-bound Soviet 163rd and 44th Divisions into isolated pockets, known as motti, and destroyed them, capturing large amounts of equipment. The victory became a symbol of Finnish resistance against a far larger enemy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-suomussalmi.jpg" alt="Battle of Suomussalmi" width="320" height="248" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edward J. Krasnobowski, Frank Martini · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_suomussalmi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Soviet objective was to advance to Oulu and cut <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> in half. On 30 November 1939 the Soviet 163rd Rifle Division under Zelentsov crossed the border near Juntusranta and at Raate and advanced towards Suomussalmi. Finnish battalions fought it from 4 December, but Suomussalmi was captured on 7 December. Colonel Hjalmar Siilasvuo's Regiment JR 27 arrived by 9 December and stopped the Soviet advance towards Hyrynsalmi, and by the 11th it had cut the Soviet road link at Raate.</p><p>Over the next two weeks Siilasvuo contained the Soviets while Finnish artillery arrived, and on 25 December his forces became the 9th Division. On 21 December Finnish raiding parties attacked the Soviet 44th Division on the Raate road. Surrounded, Zelentsov tried to break out on 24 and 25 December and was stopped. On 27 December Siilasvuo attacked from several sides, and on 28 December Soviet resistance collapsed: the 163rd Division was reduced to a few hundred men who fled towards the border. The battle lasted until 8 January 1940.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>On the Raate–Suomussalmi road the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finns">Finns</a> captured 43 tanks, 70 field guns, 278 trucks, 1,170 horses, 29 anti-tank guns, 300 machine guns and 6,000 rifles. Had the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> taken Oulu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> would have had to fight on two fronts and a rail link to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> would have been cut. Estimates of Soviet casualties vary: Allen Chew gave 22,500, David Campbell 22,500 to 27,000 and Brian Best 27,000 killed and 2,100 captured. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvar-aalto">Alvar Aalto</a> sculpted a memorial to the Finnish dead.</p><p>The battle is often cited as an example of a small force fighting on familiar ground and defeating a larger enemy. Finnish troops on skis and sleds were more mobile, while Soviet heavy equipment kept to roads in forested marshland, where the network was mostly logging trails. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finns">Finns</a> targeted Soviet field kitchens and intercepted Soviet telephone communications, and were better equipped for the cold.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Suomussalmi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Outnumbered Finnish troops, fighting on skis in forests at temperatures far below freezing, destroyed Soviet divisions at <b>Suomussalmi</b> and held the isthmus until February 1940.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-finland">Republic of Finland · Military</a></li></ul></section>
